=== HINT 1: Register + remove ===
PVOID handler = AddVectoredExceptionHandler(1, lazy_alloc_handler);
...
RemoveVectoredExceptionHandler(handler);

=== HINT 2: Inside the handler ===
You are already given the matching logic (write access violation at
LAZY_ADDR). Replace the TODO return with:
  PVOID page = VirtualAlloc(LAZY_ADDR, 4096,
                            MEM_COMMIT | MEM_RESERVE, PAGE_READWRITE);
  if (page) {
      g_veh_fired = 1;
      return EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_EXECUTION;   // the faulting write retries
  }
  return EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_SEARCH;

=== HINT 3 (KEY INSIGHT) ===
EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_EXECUTION rewinds to the faulting instruction and re-runs
it - this time the page exists, so *LAZY_ADDR = 42 succeeds. This is exactly
how lazy allocators, guard pages and pool allocators work, and how emulators
map memory on demand. An unhandled AV (wrong handler, wrong address check)
crashes the process: VEH is your first and only line of defense before SEH.
